WEBVTT

NOTE SE201 Lesson 7: Skills as building blocks — captions generated from the narration script

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.872
Software engineering 201. Lesson seven.

2
00:00:03.872 --> 00:00:06.120
Skills as building blocks.

3
00:00:06.470 --> 00:00:10.396
Why would three capable agents do the same job three different ways?

4
00:00:10.396 --> 00:00:12.994
Take our made-up subscription-billing system.

5
00:00:12.994 --> 00:00:16.342
Now and then, someone asks for a new plan, or a new price.

6
00:00:16.342 --> 00:00:20.326
The first time, an agent works out the four steps, and gets it right.

7
00:00:20.326 --> 00:00:24.771
The second time, a different agent starts from zero, and skips the tax setup.

8
00:00:24.771 --> 00:00:31.007
The third time, another one edits the old price in place, and every existing subscriber is on the new price.

9
00:00:31.007 --> 00:00:33.720
Same job. Three results. Nobody wrote down how.

10
00:00:34.070 --> 00:00:37.095
Software keeps finding new units of reuse.

11
00:00:37.095 --> 00:00:41.633
Libraries: shared code. Frameworks: an application's structure.

12
00:00:41.633 --> 00:00:46.531
Services: something running, behind an API. All three reuse software.

13
00:00:46.531 --> 00:00:50.565
A skill reuses something different: how a job gets done.

14
00:00:50.565 --> 00:00:54.310
One recurring job, written down, tested, and shared.

15
00:00:54.660 --> 00:01:00.162
Several agent tools now share a format for this: a folder, with an instruction file.

16
00:01:00.162 --> 00:01:05.075
It starts with when to use it: someone asks for a new plan, or a new price.

17
00:01:05.075 --> 00:01:07.826
That line is how an agent finds the skill.

18
00:01:07.826 --> 00:01:11.559
Then the inputs: name, price, billing period, start date.

19
00:01:11.559 --> 00:01:13.786
If one is missing, the agent asks.

20
00:01:13.786 --> 00:01:14.900
It doesn't guess.

21
00:01:15.240 --> 00:01:20.042
Then the steps, in order. Add the plan, with its price as a new version.

22
00:01:20.042 --> 00:01:23.644
Set its tax category. Map it in the accounting export.

23
00:01:23.644 --> 00:01:28.780
Then open a pull request, because a price is money, and a person approves it.

24
00:01:29.140 --> 00:01:32.447
Then the checks: how the agent knows the job worked.

25
00:01:32.447 --> 00:01:37.471
A test renewal on staging charges the right amount, tax included, exactly once.

26
00:01:37.471 --> 00:01:42.685
The ledger still adds up. And existing subscribers still renew at their old price.

27
00:01:42.685 --> 00:01:45.420
If one fails, the agent stops, and says so.

28
00:01:45.760 --> 00:01:48.267
And the traps: what went wrong before.

29
00:01:48.267 --> 00:01:50.114
Never edit a price in place.

30
00:01:50.114 --> 00:01:56.250
Add a new version. That's the mistake from the third run, written down, so nobody repeats it.

31
00:01:56.610 --> 00:01:58.859
If that sounds like a runbook, it is.

32
00:01:58.859 --> 00:02:01.351
Engineers have written those for decades.

33
00:02:01.351 --> 00:02:03.113
What's changed is the reader.

34
00:02:03.113 --> 00:02:07.367
A person reads a runbook now and then, and fills the gaps from memory.

35
00:02:07.367 --> 00:02:11.379
An agent reads the skill on every run, with no memory of the last.

36
00:02:11.379 --> 00:02:13.627
Where the page is silent, it guesses.

37
00:02:13.627 --> 00:02:17.760
And when a step needs no judgment, make it a script the skill calls.

38
00:02:18.110 --> 00:02:23.852
So treat a skill like code. Keep it in the repository, versioned, and reviewed when it changes.

39
00:02:23.852 --> 00:02:29.473
And test it. Hand it to a fresh agent that has never seen the job, and let it run on staging.

40
00:02:29.473 --> 00:02:33.160
If it can't finish from the page alone, the skill isn't done.

41
00:02:33.510 --> 00:02:36.609
Now run those three jobs again, from the skill.

42
00:02:36.609 --> 00:02:40.367
Same steps. Same checks. No guarantee: agents still vary.

43
00:02:40.367 --> 00:02:42.410
That's what the checks are for.

44
00:02:42.410 --> 00:02:45.707
And when a run goes wrong, don't just fix the run.

45
00:02:45.707 --> 00:02:50.256
Fix the skill. One change, in one place, and every later run gets it.

46
00:02:50.256 --> 00:02:53.750
That's a building block: reliable enough to build on.

47
00:02:54.100 --> 00:02:56.014
So when is a job worth a skill?

48
00:02:56.014 --> 00:03:00.211
The first time? The third? Or never, because agents can work it out?

49
00:03:00.211 --> 00:03:02.495
A good rule of thumb: the third time.

50
00:03:02.495 --> 00:03:05.581
Once, you're learning. Twice, you see the pattern.

51
00:03:05.581 --> 00:03:07.433
The third time, write it down.

52
00:03:07.433 --> 00:03:09.470
Sooner, if a mistake costs money.

53
00:03:09.820 --> 00:03:15.190
So, to recap. One recurring job, written down, with its checks and its traps.

54
00:03:15.190 --> 00:03:17.771
In the repo, tested by a fresh agent.

55
00:03:17.771 --> 00:03:20.630
And when a run goes wrong, fix the skill.

56
00:03:20.970 --> 00:03:23.714
Which gives us rule seven, for your agents.

57
00:03:23.714 --> 00:03:30.477
When a job comes back a third time, write it as a skill: when to use it, inputs, steps, checks, and traps.

58
00:03:30.477 --> 00:03:33.795
Keep it in the repo, and test it with a fresh agent.

59
00:03:33.795 --> 00:03:37.560
And when a run goes wrong, fix the skill, not just the run.

60
00:03:37.910 --> 00:03:42.296
Now, your turn. Pick a job you've explained to your AI more than twice.

61
00:03:42.296 --> 00:03:48.720
Ask it to draft the skill. Then open a fresh chat, give it only that page, and see if the job gets done.

62
00:03:49.070 --> 00:03:55.486
One skill does one job. But real work is a chain of jobs, and some of it should start on its own.

63
00:03:55.486 --> 00:03:58.793
That's next: routines, pipelines, and integration.

64
00:03:58.793 --> 00:04:00.380
See you in lesson eight.
